WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.339 --> 00:00:03.120
Artists and creators throughout history

00:00:03.120 --> 00:00:06.520
have spoken of inspiration
practically from the cradle,

00:00:06.520 --> 00:00:09.240
a tendency toward writing
or sculpture or music

00:00:09.240 --> 00:00:11.100
from before they could speak.

00:00:11.100 --> 00:00:14.430
Sharmeen Obaid Chinoy is
not one of those artists.

00:00:14.430 --> 00:00:16.810
I was 21. I never studied film.

00:00:16.810 --> 00:00:18.570
I've studied economics and government.

00:00:18.570 --> 00:00:23.570
So I literally typed into this
search bar visual journalism.

00:00:23.640 --> 00:00:25.790
And the word documentary films popped up,

00:00:25.790 --> 00:00:27.690
and I was like, that's it.

00:00:27.690 --> 00:00:29.820
I'm gonna be a documentary filmmaker.

00:00:29.820 --> 00:00:31.590
Her films have won Academy Awards,

00:00:31.590 --> 00:00:33.520
Emmys and numerous other honors.

00:00:33.520 --> 00:00:35.870
She's become one of the
most influential advocates

00:00:35.870 --> 00:00:37.740
for human rights around the world,

00:00:37.740 --> 00:00:40.190
focusing specifically
on the plight of women,

00:00:40.190 --> 00:00:43.100
in her home country of
Pakistan, and beyond.

00:00:43.100 --> 00:00:45.380
I spoke with Sharmeen
about her insatiable desire

00:00:45.380 --> 00:00:46.860
to speak truth to power,

00:00:46.860 --> 00:00:49.300
and the power that comes
from listening closely

00:00:49.300 --> 00:00:51.760
to those whose stories need to be told.

00:00:51.760 --> 00:00:56.030
Women are realizing that they
want a say in their lives,

00:00:56.030 --> 00:00:58.770
that they want to see in how
the government treats them,

00:00:58.770 --> 00:01:01.740
that they want to see in having more space

00:01:01.740 --> 00:01:02.883
in the public sphere.

00:01:04.603 --> 00:01:05.787
And men are afraid.

00:01:16.370 --> 00:01:18.650
Tell me about growing up,
your growing up years.

00:01:18.650 --> 00:01:22.140
Because from everything that
I've read and heard about you,

00:01:22.140 --> 00:01:23.170
it's so formative.

00:01:23.170 --> 00:01:24.960
Your family is so formative.

00:01:24.960 --> 00:01:29.850
From a very young age,
something was instilled in you.

00:01:29.850 --> 00:01:31.990
I grew up in Karachi, Pakistan.

00:01:31.990 --> 00:01:35.600
And, you know, I'm the
eldest of six children,

00:01:35.600 --> 00:01:36.550
five of them girls.

00:01:38.250 --> 00:01:39.510
I grew up in a family

00:01:39.510 --> 00:01:42.770
that wanted the daughters
to have an education.

00:01:42.770 --> 00:01:44.500
We were free to speak our minds.

00:01:44.500 --> 00:01:48.470
And that was very encouraging.

00:01:48.470 --> 00:01:52.450
From a very young age, I
realized that I had grown up

00:01:52.450 --> 00:01:55.940
on the side of the tracks
where I was very lucky.

00:01:55.940 --> 00:01:59.160
And I remember one particular instance

00:01:59.160 --> 00:02:01.880
when I was about 10 years
old and I was driving,

00:02:01.880 --> 00:02:03.040
I was going to school

00:02:03.040 --> 00:02:05.800
and my car had stopped at a traffic light.

00:02:05.800 --> 00:02:07.600
And I remember looking out of the window

00:02:07.600 --> 00:02:09.380
and I saw a young girl,

00:02:09.380 --> 00:02:10.880
she was about 10 years old

00:02:10.880 --> 00:02:14.437
and she was barefoot and
she had her hand pressed out

00:02:14.437 --> 00:02:17.100
and the nose was glued
to the window screen

00:02:17.100 --> 00:02:17.980
and she was begging.

00:02:17.980 --> 00:02:20.560
And I remember thinking,
why is she not in school?

00:02:20.560 --> 00:02:23.470
And I always had these
questions, you know,

00:02:23.470 --> 00:02:24.890
like, why is this not happening?

00:02:24.890 --> 00:02:26.190
Why is that not happening?

00:02:27.360 --> 00:02:31.020
Pakistan is a society
which frowns upon questions

00:02:31.020 --> 00:02:32.460
that are probing.

00:02:32.460 --> 00:02:34.850
You know, you're supposed
to accept the status quo.

00:02:34.850 --> 00:02:36.520
This is just the way it is.

00:02:36.520 --> 00:02:39.430
Don't talk about this. It'll bring shame.

00:02:39.430 --> 00:02:41.750
And so I was that annoying kid in class

00:02:41.750 --> 00:02:43.210
who would put up their hand all the time

00:02:43.210 --> 00:02:44.780
and ask difficult questions.

00:02:44.780 --> 00:02:46.740
And at home I did that as well.

00:02:46.740 --> 00:02:49.527
And my mother was raising
five children was like,

00:02:49.527 --> 00:02:52.840
you need to ask someone
else these questions.

00:02:52.840 --> 00:02:54.680
And she encouraged me to write.

00:02:54.680 --> 00:02:58.070
And I became a writer when
I was about 14 years old.

00:02:58.070 --> 00:02:59.820
And I think I've never looked back.

00:03:01.320 --> 00:03:03.270
Sharmeen's innate drive to ask questions

00:03:03.270 --> 00:03:06.090
and seek the answers led
her to the United States

00:03:06.090 --> 00:03:09.110
were she pursued her higher
education at Smith College,

00:03:09.110 --> 00:03:12.040
that was despite her
family's initial reluctance.

00:03:12.040 --> 00:03:14.330
Although she studied economics
and political science,

00:03:14.330 --> 00:03:15.530
her experiences at Smith

00:03:15.530 --> 00:03:18.370
ultimately led her down a different path.

00:03:18.370 --> 00:03:22.170
You know, when I was on
campus, I had a lot of sit ins.

00:03:22.170 --> 00:03:24.810
I was a very, I was involved
in student government.

00:03:24.810 --> 00:03:27.060
I was junior class president.

00:03:27.060 --> 00:03:29.670
I was very politically active.

00:03:29.670 --> 00:03:32.210
And it gave me an opportunity

00:03:32.210 --> 00:03:34.550
to meet people from around the world,

00:03:34.550 --> 00:03:38.963
women who came from Africa and
Asia and the United States,

00:03:39.810 --> 00:03:43.470
who, if you think, would have
had different experiences.

00:03:43.470 --> 00:03:44.603
But let me tell you,

00:03:45.590 --> 00:03:47.880
women around the world
share a lot in common.

00:03:47.880 --> 00:03:50.520
It doesn't matter where they come from.

00:03:50.520 --> 00:03:53.540
There is an inherent bias against women

00:03:53.540 --> 00:03:57.530
that exists in almost
every society in the world,

00:03:57.530 --> 00:03:59.010
whether it's in the north or the east

00:03:59.010 --> 00:04:00.380
or the south of the west.

00:04:00.380 --> 00:04:04.370
And I think that was a
transformative period for me

00:04:04.370 --> 00:04:08.810
as a individual, because
right out of Smith,

00:04:08.810 --> 00:04:11.460
I decided to take up
documentary filmmaking

00:04:11.460 --> 00:04:12.807
and switch my career.

00:04:13.940 --> 00:04:16.310
All right, so on that note,
you give me a great opening

00:04:16.310 --> 00:04:18.620
to talk about what I
think is a pivotal moment.

00:04:18.620 --> 00:04:19.490
I think you'll agree.

00:04:19.490 --> 00:04:21.300
And I got a chance to catch up

00:04:21.300 --> 00:04:23.590
with your colleague and friend Trish.

00:04:23.590 --> 00:04:26.200
And she talked about meeting
you for the first time

00:04:26.200 --> 00:04:28.920
and a pivotal moment that you have

00:04:28.920 --> 00:04:31.930
with a very well known news organization

00:04:31.930 --> 00:04:33.230
here in the United States.

00:04:34.870 --> 00:04:36.980
When we met, it was in a coffee shop

00:04:36.980 --> 00:04:41.980
and it was like realizing
that we shared the same goal

00:04:42.130 --> 00:04:45.830
of finding voices that
weren't being heard,

00:04:45.830 --> 00:04:47.560
giving them a voice, giving them a light,

00:04:47.560 --> 00:04:49.150
and we were young at the time

00:04:49.150 --> 00:04:51.210
and we didn't have connections.

00:04:51.210 --> 00:04:54.850
And Sharmeen, like, marched
into the New York Times office

00:04:54.850 --> 00:04:55.960
and said, you know, "I have a story

00:04:55.960 --> 00:04:57.410
and I want to make a film."

00:04:57.410 --> 00:05:00.970
And, you know, it was kind
of an outlandish thing to do

00:05:00.970 --> 00:05:02.210
in your early 20s.

00:05:02.210 --> 00:05:03.990
And she was able to do it.

00:05:03.990 --> 00:05:07.110
And she went and she made
films on the front lines

00:05:07.110 --> 00:05:09.110
of a lot of war zones in the Middle East

00:05:09.110 --> 00:05:11.750
and came back and delivered these stories

00:05:11.750 --> 00:05:14.940
from people who are
suffering from injustice

00:05:14.940 --> 00:05:16.510
and whose voices weren't being heard.

00:05:16.510 --> 00:05:17.810
And they made it to New York Times

00:05:17.810 --> 00:05:19.790
and reached an international audience.

00:05:19.790 --> 00:05:22.180
So that's a big moment, obviously.

00:05:22.180 --> 00:05:23.130
Yes, it was.

00:05:23.130 --> 00:05:25.320
You know, I was 21 years old

00:05:25.320 --> 00:05:27.760
and I was writing for these newspapers,

00:05:27.760 --> 00:05:29.800
and 9/11 had happened,

00:05:29.800 --> 00:05:31.410
and I would watch the nightly news

00:05:31.410 --> 00:05:32.670
and I would see all these people

00:05:32.670 --> 00:05:36.710
catch up into South Asia and
into Afghanistan and Pakistan

00:05:36.710 --> 00:05:38.840
and with very little understanding

00:05:38.840 --> 00:05:42.110
of what the politics of the region were

00:05:42.110 --> 00:05:43.090
and what was happening.

00:05:43.090 --> 00:05:45.370
And I thought to myself,
I can tell stories

00:05:45.370 --> 00:05:48.600
from that part of the world
to this part of the world.

00:05:48.600 --> 00:05:52.020
And I've wrote up this
proposal for my first film.

00:05:52.020 --> 00:05:53.180
I wanted to do a film

00:05:53.180 --> 00:05:56.270
about the impact of the war in Afghanistan

00:05:56.270 --> 00:05:58.140
on the youngest victims of the war,

00:05:58.140 --> 00:06:00.160
which were the children of Afghanistan.

00:06:00.160 --> 00:06:04.720
And I sent it off to about,
I mean, 80 organizations

00:06:04.720 --> 00:06:05.670
in the United States.

00:06:05.670 --> 00:06:08.180
Every production house I could find.

00:06:08.180 --> 00:06:11.210
One day online, I found the e-mail address

00:06:11.210 --> 00:06:14.200
of the president of the then
New York Times television.

00:06:14.200 --> 00:06:17.780
And I emailed him and,
you know, 15 minutes later

00:06:17.780 --> 00:06:20.129
he responded and I bought my first suit

00:06:20.129 --> 00:06:22.646
and got on an Amtrak train
and went to New York.

00:06:22.646 --> 00:06:24.734
And I pitched it to the editorial board

00:06:24.734 --> 00:06:25.670
of The New York Times.

00:06:25.670 --> 00:06:29.430
And they funded my first
film, my first feature film.

00:06:40.490 --> 00:06:42.250
Sharmeen spent her first 15 films

00:06:42.250 --> 00:06:44.590
in front of the camera,
amplifying powerful stories

00:06:44.590 --> 00:06:47.400
through her own experience,
and with her own voice.

00:06:47.400 --> 00:06:49.610
By 2011, she'd decided to put the focus

00:06:49.610 --> 00:06:52.570
squarely on the lives and
struggles of her subjects.

00:06:52.570 --> 00:06:55.370
That led to her breakthrough
film, "Saving Face,"

00:06:55.370 --> 00:06:56.570
about women in Pakistan

00:06:56.570 --> 00:06:58.830
who've been the victims of acid attacks.

00:06:58.830 --> 00:07:00.880
The film earned her an Academy Award,

00:07:00.880 --> 00:07:03.400
the first ever won by a Pakistani.

00:07:03.400 --> 00:07:06.490
To all the women in Pakistan,
we're working for change.

00:07:06.490 --> 00:07:07.940
Don't give up on your dreams.

00:07:07.940 --> 00:07:08.893
This is for you.

00:07:15.500 --> 00:07:20.500
I immersed myself in a
world where so many women

00:07:20.548 --> 00:07:22.770
literally were disfigured

00:07:22.770 --> 00:07:26.780
simply because they were
brave enough to say no,

00:07:26.780 --> 00:07:29.653
no to violence.

00:07:30.760 --> 00:07:33.870
When acid is thrown on your face,

00:07:33.870 --> 00:07:37.400
your entire face carries
that mark forever.

00:07:37.400 --> 00:07:38.520
When you wake up in the morning

00:07:38.520 --> 00:07:39.880
and you see your face in the mirror,

00:07:39.880 --> 00:07:42.550
you're reminded of that incident.

00:07:42.550 --> 00:07:44.480
And telling this story was so important

00:07:44.480 --> 00:07:46.800
because the legislation in Pakistan

00:07:46.800 --> 00:07:49.320
was so weak around acid
violence at that time

00:07:49.320 --> 00:07:52.060
that Daniel and I felt
that this was a story

00:07:52.060 --> 00:07:52.920
we needed to tell.

00:07:52.920 --> 00:07:54.940
We need to impact legislation

00:07:54.940 --> 00:07:58.820
and in fact, the way
people saw acid violence

00:07:58.820 --> 00:07:59.653
in the country.

00:08:02.310 --> 00:08:06.310
I think the moment for me
in this film was when Zakia,

00:08:06.310 --> 00:08:10.600
who is a woman who had had
acid thrown on her face

00:08:10.600 --> 00:08:14.513
by her husband because
she wanted a divorce.

00:08:15.660 --> 00:08:18.841
I remember walking in to
film her one morning and,

00:08:18.841 --> 00:08:22.270
you know, half of her face was gone,

00:08:22.270 --> 00:08:23.780
it had been disfigured

00:08:23.780 --> 00:08:26.300
and she was peeling
apples for her daughter

00:08:28.120 --> 00:08:29.700
and she was smiling

00:08:29.700 --> 00:08:31.600
and they were telling jokes to each other

00:08:31.600 --> 00:08:34.720
about cats and kittens.

00:08:34.720 --> 00:08:35.750
And they were both laughing.

00:08:35.750 --> 00:08:38.140
And in that mother
daughter moment, I realized

00:08:38.140 --> 00:08:42.620
that these are some of the
bravest women that I have met,

00:08:42.620 --> 00:08:46.950
because through this ordeal,
they have laugh and joke

00:08:46.950 --> 00:08:49.780
and not forgotten that
they are, after all,

00:08:49.780 --> 00:08:51.800
mothers and human beings.

00:08:51.800 --> 00:08:54.260
And that's why that story really changed

00:08:54.260 --> 00:08:58.330
the way I saw the strength of a woman.

00:08:58.330 --> 00:09:03.330
And that clearly vault you to
a new level in your career.

00:09:03.740 --> 00:09:07.690
You've become much better known globally

00:09:07.690 --> 00:09:10.100
and also in your own country,

00:09:10.100 --> 00:09:11.820
where obviously you're
well known at that point,

00:09:11.820 --> 00:09:13.762
but this is a new level

00:09:13.762 --> 00:09:16.540
and things start to accelerate for you.

00:09:16.540 --> 00:09:21.110
Does it become harder or easier
as you gain more notoriety?

00:09:21.110 --> 00:09:23.560
Pakistan does not take to well,

00:09:23.560 --> 00:09:26.020
to having a woman who has a voice

00:09:26.020 --> 00:09:27.593
and who's not afraid to do so.

00:09:29.110 --> 00:09:33.413
And I think that when I
won my first Academy Award,

00:09:35.050 --> 00:09:37.580
people suddenly realized
that I had a voice

00:09:37.580 --> 00:09:41.220
and there were many who
wanted to silence it

00:09:41.220 --> 00:09:43.350
because I was taking up issues

00:09:43.350 --> 00:09:45.510
that they didn't want to taken up,

00:09:45.510 --> 00:09:48.890
especially not on the global level.

00:09:48.890 --> 00:09:52.170
And, you know, I would
wake up some mornings

00:09:52.170 --> 00:09:53.850
and I would read social media

00:09:53.850 --> 00:09:56.110
and I would learn some things about myself

00:09:56.110 --> 00:09:57.573
I didn't even know were true.

00:09:58.530 --> 00:10:02.020
And so, you know, there
were massive campaigns

00:10:02.020 --> 00:10:03.783
that were started to discredit me.

00:10:04.720 --> 00:10:06.810
But, you know, none of it can stick

00:10:06.810 --> 00:10:11.810
because all I'm doing is
telling stories that are,

00:10:12.500 --> 00:10:15.000
real stories that need to be told.

00:10:16.370 --> 00:10:18.350
It's interesting that
we're talking about this

00:10:18.350 --> 00:10:22.980
because I want to bring in
another interview about you

00:10:22.980 --> 00:10:25.103
to have you watch with your friend Marvy.

00:10:26.030 --> 00:10:27.910
Marvi Mazhar is a prominent architect

00:10:27.910 --> 00:10:30.040
based in Karachi, Pakistan.

00:10:30.040 --> 00:10:32.587
She's bonded with Sharmeen over
the important role of women

00:10:32.587 --> 00:10:35.250
in Pakistani society.

00:10:35.250 --> 00:10:39.840
I think Pakistan is so
open to ideas right now

00:10:39.840 --> 00:10:42.660
and every different platform
from artist community,

00:10:42.660 --> 00:10:44.040
from a bureaucratic level,

00:10:44.040 --> 00:10:47.840
I feel they're giving that space to women

00:10:47.840 --> 00:10:49.890
and generally to new ideas.

00:10:49.890 --> 00:10:52.580
I feel Pakistan has a lot of potential

00:10:52.580 --> 00:10:55.180
in giving that space to others.

00:10:55.180 --> 00:10:56.350
It makes its mistakes.

00:10:56.350 --> 00:10:58.790
It has its own teething issues.

00:10:58.790 --> 00:11:01.470
But that's where people like Sharmeen

00:11:01.470 --> 00:11:03.760
bring in a lot of perspective,

00:11:03.760 --> 00:11:07.220
working with the government,
working with local diaspora

00:11:07.220 --> 00:11:08.830
and then bringing and
knitting it together.

00:11:08.830 --> 00:11:13.140
So I feel that we, all of
us are have the same vision

00:11:13.140 --> 00:11:16.280
of making Pakistan on a platform

00:11:16.280 --> 00:11:20.693
where it recognizes such
new ideas and new ventures.

00:11:21.580 --> 00:11:25.180
So she seemed when I spoke
with her, very optimistic,

00:11:25.180 --> 00:11:26.750
I think that comes
through in that comment.

00:11:26.750 --> 00:11:27.740
Do you share that?

00:11:27.740 --> 00:11:29.840
I do. I share the optimism.

00:11:29.840 --> 00:11:30.930
And I'll tell you this,

00:11:30.930 --> 00:11:33.530
that the Internet has changed Pakistan.

00:11:33.530 --> 00:11:35.630
There is a free flow of ideas.

00:11:35.630 --> 00:11:37.050
If you would ask me in my 20s

00:11:37.050 --> 00:11:40.110
would I work with the government,
I would have said never.

00:11:40.110 --> 00:11:42.190
But I do work with the
government in Pakistan

00:11:42.190 --> 00:11:44.650
on projects a lot.

00:11:44.650 --> 00:11:47.410
I currently am working with
the Ministry of Human Rights,

00:11:47.410 --> 00:11:48.950
with the Ministry of Justice.

00:11:48.950 --> 00:11:51.490
But I will also say this,

00:11:51.490 --> 00:11:55.400
that it is a country
where you have to dance,

00:11:55.400 --> 00:11:57.720
where you have to walk the fine line,

00:11:57.720 --> 00:12:02.080
where you have to think about the project

00:12:02.080 --> 00:12:04.790
that you're working on and
the personal repercussions

00:12:04.790 --> 00:12:06.520
those would have on you.

00:12:06.520 --> 00:12:10.130
And so I have learned to
navigate those worlds.

00:12:10.130 --> 00:12:12.600
And when you live in Pakistan,

00:12:12.600 --> 00:12:15.683
I think you're sort of born
with that navigation skills.

00:12:18.350 --> 00:12:19.980
As Sharmeen's influence has expanded,

00:12:19.980 --> 00:12:21.760
so has her portfolio.

00:12:21.760 --> 00:12:24.750
Her 2016 short documentary,
"A Girl in the River,"

00:12:24.750 --> 00:12:27.090
about honor killings of Pakistani women,

00:12:27.090 --> 00:12:28.710
won her a second Oscar,

00:12:28.710 --> 00:12:31.570
the first time anyone had
won twice in the category.

00:12:31.570 --> 00:12:34.470
This is what happens when
determined women get together.

00:12:36.350 --> 00:12:37.860
The film's international recognition

00:12:37.860 --> 00:12:40.590
helped inspire legislation in Pakistan

00:12:40.590 --> 00:12:42.890
that made honor killings illegal.

00:12:42.890 --> 00:12:43.800
That closed a loophole

00:12:43.800 --> 00:12:47.730
that had previously allowed
the practice to go unpunished.

00:12:47.730 --> 00:12:49.400
My film deals with forgiveness

00:12:49.400 --> 00:12:51.210
and how the law is manipulated,

00:12:51.210 --> 00:12:52.930
and now they're going to change that.

00:12:52.930 --> 00:12:54.550
She's also taken on subject matter

00:12:54.550 --> 00:12:56.830
far beyond her home country's borders,

00:12:56.830 --> 00:12:59.190
calling out what she saw as exploitation

00:12:59.190 --> 00:13:01.260
in American college sports

00:13:01.260 --> 00:13:03.190
in the documentary "Student Athlete."

00:13:03.190 --> 00:13:05.650
She's also produced a
range of animated films

00:13:05.650 --> 00:13:07.310
to broaden her audience.

00:13:07.310 --> 00:13:09.360
In addition to thinking
about what to make,

00:13:09.360 --> 00:13:11.800
she's also thinking about who's making it

00:13:11.800 --> 00:13:14.170
and who's watching the finished product.

00:13:14.170 --> 00:13:17.720
About four years ago, I
thought about my body of work.

00:13:17.720 --> 00:13:21.560
I've created so many
films. Who's watched them?

00:13:21.560 --> 00:13:24.450
You know, of course, I
have a global audience,

00:13:24.450 --> 00:13:26.860
but locally, who's been
able to watch them?

00:13:26.860 --> 00:13:29.440
The ones that are on the
Internet are still accessible

00:13:29.440 --> 00:13:31.570
to the ones who have access to Internet.

00:13:31.570 --> 00:13:33.490
And so I built a mobile cinema

00:13:33.490 --> 00:13:36.150
that goes from village
to village, town to town,

00:13:36.150 --> 00:13:38.320
showcasing not just my films,

00:13:38.320 --> 00:13:40.504
but also films of other filmmakers.

00:13:40.504 --> 00:13:44.170
And the idea was that we
would take these films

00:13:44.170 --> 00:13:47.120
into the heartland, into
the towns and villages

00:13:47.120 --> 00:13:51.250
where men and women and children
together could watch films

00:13:51.250 --> 00:13:53.103
and have discussions afterwards.

00:13:54.780 --> 00:13:57.070
And our films range from, for women,

00:13:57.070 --> 00:14:00.050
how to inherit property
or how to get a divorce,

00:14:00.050 --> 00:14:01.790
or if they're battered, you know,

00:14:01.790 --> 00:14:03.750
which is a shelter, they can go to,

00:14:03.750 --> 00:14:05.540
those informational films.

00:14:05.540 --> 00:14:07.645
I feel like the choices I'm making

00:14:07.645 --> 00:14:10.890
have a lot to do with ensuring
that the next generation

00:14:10.890 --> 00:14:14.140
in my country has the
opportunities that I did

00:14:14.140 --> 00:14:17.530
by exposing them to thoughts and ideas

00:14:17.530 --> 00:14:19.610
and competing worldviews.

00:14:19.610 --> 00:14:20.680
You've said something

00:14:20.680 --> 00:14:23.870
that I wanted to get you to
talk a little bit more about,

00:14:23.870 --> 00:14:26.270
which is men watching your films.

00:14:26.270 --> 00:14:30.810
You know, globally, I feel
like men of all nationalities

00:14:30.810 --> 00:14:35.810
are reckoning with our history as a sex,

00:14:36.520 --> 00:14:40.810
our history as it relates to women.

00:14:40.810 --> 00:14:44.470
How has your relationship
with the male population,

00:14:44.470 --> 00:14:47.520
as it were, if you can
generalize, changed?

00:14:47.520 --> 00:14:52.480
There are men in Pakistan who abhor me,

00:14:52.480 --> 00:14:54.180
who cannot stand me,

00:14:54.180 --> 00:14:59.170
who think that I should be silenced.

00:14:59.170 --> 00:15:03.270
And that's because they're afraid

00:15:03.270 --> 00:15:06.330
that women like myself
will take back power,

00:15:06.330 --> 00:15:11.330
that we will no longer
be seeking permission,

00:15:11.750 --> 00:15:13.313
that we will just grab it.

00:15:15.390 --> 00:15:18.180
And at the same time, I have a lot of men

00:15:18.180 --> 00:15:20.680
who come up to me at airports,

00:15:20.680 --> 00:15:23.727
who come up to me at
public spaces and say,

00:15:23.727 --> 00:15:26.880
"I want my daughter to be just like you.

00:15:26.880 --> 00:15:28.660
I want her to be free.

00:15:28.660 --> 00:15:31.540
I want her to be able to speak her mind."

00:15:31.540 --> 00:15:34.410
So as Pakistan is a
society that is changing,

00:15:34.410 --> 00:15:36.630
there will be people who
will love me and my work

00:15:36.630 --> 00:15:40.570
and there will be people who
will dislike me and my work.

00:15:40.570 --> 00:15:42.370
But I choose to hear the voices

00:15:42.370 --> 00:15:44.260
that cheer me on to the finish line

00:15:44.260 --> 00:15:47.460
and not the ones that
want me to be silenced.

00:15:47.460 --> 00:15:49.850
You know, it strikes
me, as you're speaking,

00:15:49.850 --> 00:15:53.920
that you're going back to
this this idea of balance

00:15:53.920 --> 00:15:57.210
and in an interview you gave,

00:15:57.210 --> 00:16:01.730
you talked about your desire and your need

00:16:01.730 --> 00:16:06.730
to provoke anger sometimes
because anger leads to action.

00:16:06.750 --> 00:16:09.770
And yet at the same time,
it feels like there's a tone

00:16:09.770 --> 00:16:11.120
through so much of your work

00:16:11.120 --> 00:16:13.760
that is about hope and encouragement.

00:16:13.760 --> 00:16:17.590
And I do wonder how
you strike that balance

00:16:17.590 --> 00:16:19.570
and how you go about doing that daily,

00:16:19.570 --> 00:16:22.506
how you go about doing
that as you choose projects

00:16:22.506 --> 00:16:23.993
and make films.

00:16:25.030 --> 00:16:28.750
I think that anger is a
really important emotion,

00:16:28.750 --> 00:16:30.300
because when you're angry,

00:16:30.300 --> 00:16:33.720
then you're moved to discuss issues,

00:16:33.720 --> 00:16:36.960
you're moved to push legislation through,

00:16:36.960 --> 00:16:40.140
you're moved to ensure that that injustice

00:16:40.140 --> 00:16:42.600
does not happen to anyone else.

00:16:42.600 --> 00:16:44.220
But I think it's equally important

00:16:44.220 --> 00:16:46.070
in the world that we live in today

00:16:46.070 --> 00:16:50.010
when a 24 hour news cycle to have hope,

00:16:50.010 --> 00:16:52.220
because if you don't have hope,

00:16:52.220 --> 00:16:55.720
then you don't have a
desire to create change.

00:16:55.720 --> 00:16:58.160
And so it's important
for me to show the wins

00:16:58.160 --> 00:17:01.220
as much as it is important
for me to show the losses.

00:17:01.220 --> 00:17:03.070
And I think it was a few years ago,

00:17:03.070 --> 00:17:05.070
I decided that I no longer wanted

00:17:05.070 --> 00:17:07.000
to talk about issues.

00:17:07.000 --> 00:17:09.200
I wanted to talk about the champions

00:17:09.200 --> 00:17:12.300
who were creating change,

00:17:12.300 --> 00:17:15.040
who were working on those issues.

00:17:15.040 --> 00:17:18.210
We understand that that issue is enormous.

00:17:18.210 --> 00:17:21.980
We understand that it
probably outlive our lifetime,

00:17:21.980 --> 00:17:24.420
but that someone is working on that.

00:17:24.420 --> 00:17:25.850
And that gives us hope

00:17:25.850 --> 00:17:27.890
that you're chipping away at something

00:17:27.890 --> 00:17:29.093
day by day by day.

